 # Primitive Geometry and Distribution
 
-Also called: Primitive Representation Family
 
 ## Overview
-This family defines the geometric primitives used to represent 3D scenes in differentiable splatting methods, extending the standard 3D Gaussian ellipsoid to alternative shapes, distributions, and dimensionalities. It solves the limitations of volumetric Gaussian mixtures, such as ambiguous geometry, poor mesh extraction, and inefficient representation of sharp edges or opaque surfaces, by introducing planar disks, fat-tailed distributions, generalized exponential functions, and polyhedral volumes [S1, S2, S3]. It differs from sibling methods by varying the fundamental density function or geometric support of the primitive while maintaining the core splatting pipeline of projection and alpha compositing.
+This family defines the geometric primitives used to represent 3D scenes in differentiable splatting methods, extending the standard 3D Gaussian ellipsoid to alternative shapes, distributions, and dimensionalities [G]. It addresses specific limitations of standard volumetric representations by introducing specialized primitives: planar disks for surface reconstruction [2D Gaussian Splatting](6068-2d-gaussian-splatting.md), fat-tailed distributions for non-monotonic density modeling [3D Student's t Distribution Splatting](4352-3d-student-s-t-distribution-splatting.md), generalized exponential functions for high-frequency content [Generalized Exponential Splatting](4684-generalized-exponential-splatting.md), and transparent polyhedra for linear volumetric rendering [LinPrim Octahedron Representation]. It differs from sibling methods by varying the fundamental density function or geometric support of the primitive while maintaining the core splatting pipeline of projection and alpha compositing [G].
 
